A||N||25||FieroJoe||555 Timer IC being really sensitive...(long)||||5||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||05-16-2001||10:35 PM||||Well, for those of you who remember my "Stupid Electronics" ( <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/001614.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/001614.html</A>  ) topic about a timer circuit I was working on, it finally crapped out on me and I went to experimenting with a 555 IC.  The "analog" one was not timing reliably, and sometimes would never shut off.<P>So I built the 555 circuit in monostable mode, got it working so I can have it time-on for about 7 seconds, and installed it in my car.<P>The way the old one was set up, was that whenever the doors were unlocked or locked, the locking relay sends a +12v signal to the timer circuit to trigger it.  I also had a hidden pushbutton that would also trigger the circuit, also giving +12v.<P>So, I intended on having the 555 circuit triggered in the same means, but when I was bench testing it, I ran into a few weird things.<P>Here are some pics to aid in my description:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/555timerfront.jpg">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/555timerback.jpg">  <P>So the wierd thing that's going on, is when I have +12V and ground connected to it (terminals 1 and 2), I dont even have to give terminal 3 +12v to trigger it, all I have to do is touch it.  I can just tap it with a screw driver and then it sets off.  Now, I know that there is a very very minute amount of electrons moving from the timer into the screwdriver (maybe .00000001 Coulumbs   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">), but it seems to be enough to set the darn thing off.<P>Knowing that only a connection needed to be made to set the thing off, I just wired up the pushbutton switch that when it was pushed, a connection was made from terminal 3 to 2, and so the timer would be set.  I now had the intention of hooking up those two trigger wires from the power locks, so it would go when the locks were fired, as the old one did.  I hooked them up, pushed an unlock button, and it timed, and timed, and timed, and never stopped.  So I tested the trigger wires for voltage "at rest", and there was some, so I knew that was my problem.  One of the locking relays must be leaking voltage.  But no problem, I would fix that later.  It was getting late, and I wanted to drive the car the next day so I put everything together (its mounted under driveer's side dash speaker), with the only way to set my timer by pushing my button.<P>So, I get everything back together, unlock my doors so I can get out of the car, and then the timer started!!  Perplexed, I tried it again, this time with the lock.  yup, it started timing again.  Started pushing other things, and a few other electrical circuits in my car will get this thing to go, magically.  Horn, left power mirror (not right, only left), headlights, and of course lock/unlock.<P>So what the heck?  Enough electrical noise from those circuits causing this thing to trip?  How do I fix it?  I was thinking of either a highpass or a lowpass capacitor-resistor filter, but didnt know if it would work.<P>Sorry for the long winded post, but does anyone have any suggestions on how to get this to work only when I want it to, not when other things trip it?<P>Thanks!!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg">  <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87<p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Taijiguy||05-16-2001||11:54 PM||||It looks from the picture like you don't have a filter cap on the supply voltage, or the trigger. You may need to add one on each of those. I seem to remember some time ago making a touch switch, that used the 555 to sample a metal pad, and to latch a relay until the pad was touched again. It sounds like you have something similar happening there. I'll have to look for the circuit if I still have it.<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||RNGCRSH||05-17-2001||12:29 AM||||Tie the trigger to ground with a resistor 100K ohms or so. What you have is the trigger is floating to a value only slighty less than the actual trigger point. when you touch it with anything, stray fields cause it to trigger. give it a try I think it will solve your problem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eric_PA||05-17-2001||01:00 PM||||Definately use a pull-down resistor. You most likely have a floating input on that circuit. just like RNGCRSH said. I just got done with my senior design project and had the same problem using a 555 timer in the circuit. It was for a robot that followed lines on the floor. I'll post a pic of it tonight when I get home.I'm glad school is now over for me cause I have more time to respond to posts like this one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric_PA||05-17-2001||04:58 PM||||WEll here is the pic of the robot. The same 555 problem you are having was my problem on this project. Once that was nailed it worked like a charm.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/May12$04.jpg">  <P>Here is the circuit Schematic I made up for it showing the 555 Timer. The red resistor was added for the pull down.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/circuit.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||05-17-2001||10:29 PM||||Thanks for the input, I'll try that resistor as soon as I can.  Should I just put it across the screw terminals (#2 and #3), so I wouldnt have to solder anything in there?  Seems to me that that should work.<P>That robot looks really really neat.  Are you taking EE classes?  I wish I knew what some of the things were in that schmatic, or even the theory behind how it is supposed to work.  But, then again, Im just learning as I find new projects for me to make.  I hope to take some classes soon, so maybe I can get some "formal" instruction!<P>Thanks again, I'll let you know how it worked!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric_PA||05-18-2001||06:44 AM||||FieroJoe, putting a resistor across those pins would work fine. As far as the classes I'm taking, I'm working towards a BSEMT in Electro Mechanical Engineering Technology. It is offered at Penn State. I started out at a nearby Community college for electronics, and was very happy with what I have learned there. Going part time makes it rough, but well worth it.<P>Eric<p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 05-18-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000007||theogre||05-19-2001||12:37 PM||||I'm slightly fuzzy still.... I'm not sure I followed all the above....<P>I'm assuming you are using the 555 to drive a relay.... You probabaly need to add a driver transistor between it and the 555. The 555 output and allot of relays don't play well together. (It's really ment to drive a transistor or a "reed" relay that doesn't use much power.) A 2N2222 or similar low power switching transistor will handle automotive relays fine in mose cases.<P>You'll also want a shunt diode across the relay to prevent it spiking the drivers.<P>You may also want a diode and a 100mf filter on the 12v into the little board. The diode will keep spike loads in the car from sucking down the filter cap. This will give you much better power for the 555.||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroJoe||05-19-2001||06:19 PM||||Nice to see you back, ogre.  I hope all went well.<P>No, I am not using the 555 to drive a relay.  The output is connected directly to the load.  The load, at its maximum setting, will draw 27 mA or less.  I dont know whether that will be maxing it out or not, but I dont think it should.  It has acted fine so far, except for the above symptoms.<P>Right now I am going to throw in the 100k resistor and see what happens.  If that doesnt work, then I'll start messing around with filter caps.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cliff Pennock||05-19-2001||07:49 PM||||Isn't 100K a bit high for a pull-down resistor?||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroJoe||05-20-2001||02:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>Isn't 100K a bit high for a pull-down resistor?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh, actaully, the resistor just made it time infinatly.  Even though it was 100k, it still made the little connection that was needed, so the timer kept going, and going, and going, and never shutting off.  I pulled it out of the car, there isnt anything I can do while it is in the car.  I think that I am going to rebuild it and use dry transfers instead of the 22 gauge copper wire.  I dont know if my thinking is right, but maybe all the copper wire is making some wierd electromagnetic fields thats making this thing whacky.<P>Anyway, its late, and me thinks I dont want to think about it now.  thanks of all of your help.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cliff Pennock||05-20-2001||06:39 PM||||I just realized, you trigger the 555 by pulling pin 2 <B>LOW</B>, not <B>HIGH</B>. So that's your problem there.<P>Invert the trigger as follows:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/invert.gif"> <P>That should work.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cliff Pennock||05-20-2001||06:41 PM||||Ooops, I forgot to say the transistor is a BC547||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroJoe||05-21-2001||09:33 AM||||Thanks Cliff.  I just reread the online tutorial where I learned how to build it in the first place, and it reads:<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Pin 2 (Trigger):  This pin is the input to the lower comparator and is used to set the latch, which in turn causes the output to go<BR>high. This is the beginning of the timing sequence in monostable operation. Triggering is accomplished by taking the pin from<BR>above to below a voltage level of 1/3 V+ (or, in general, one-half the voltage appearing at pin 5). <BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So yes, indeed I have a problem with applying just a +12v pulse, it also needs to go low.  I think I may understand how your schematic works Cliff, but could you give me a little explanation on exactly how it will accomplish this?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Just a beginner here!<P>As for the filter caps that theogre suggested, on both the control and trigger, would those also be wise to add while I am at it?  What values should I use?<P>This is the schematic that I have been working from, I'll have to draw in the additions when I get home from school and then post it to see if I have got it right.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.uoguelph.ca/~antoon/gadgets/555fig9a.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 05-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cliff Pennock||05-21-2001||12:03 PM||||It's actually quite simple. The resistor and zener diode are only there to protect the transistor and the rest of your electronics. As soon as 12V is applied, the transistor will pull pin 2 low. I don't think there's a need to use a pull-up resistor on pin 2 since I think the pin is internally pulled high (or at least it's floating above 1/3V+).<P>As for the filter caps, it couldn't hurt but I don't think it's necessary and I even suspect it doesn't help much in a Fiero (if you want to filter out all distortions in a Fiero, believe me, you'll need a lot more filtering than just a few caps...)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||05-22-2001||11:22 AM||||The only filtering I was talking about was input power... 100mf is plenty for a 555. As Cliff said, car power is anything but clean. <P>The reason I mention a diode in the V+ feed is to prevent the car from using the filter. This way the filter only runs the chip.<P>Myself, I use a driver transistor on a 555 anytime the load on the output is more than one or 2 "gates". (I.E. transistor base lead.) Even a bright LED can push it. Superbright's definatly can be too much.<P>Also, 555 usually have 2 output currents listed... one is "source", the other is "sink". The chip will not source out as much current as it will sink(carry to ground).||||reg||1
Z||000016||Cliff Pennock||05-22-2001||01:31 PM||||Actually, a diode in the V+ feed is a very good idea to protect the circuitry.||||reg||1
Z||000017||DJRice||05-22-2001||04:46 PM||||Eric, <P>I did a similar thing for my Senior Design Project except we tracked white lines on a black board, once the bot recieved an Infrared signal from one of four "bases" located on a playing field.  It was for an IEEE hardware competition.  Actually we had 4 teams at my school compete for who would represent the school at the competition.  <P>Its a great feeling to see one of those monstrosities actually work.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||DJRice||05-22-2001||04:50 PM||||I just went back and looked at that picture and I think we had similar sensors on our robot.  Man are those things cool....So the logic behind how the bot moves is all hardwired with 74xx stuff?  We had a Motorola 68HC12 as the brains with some PWM based motor controllers for steering and speed control.  <P>Seeing that pic makes me want to break out my breadboard and go build something....<p>[This message has been edited by DJRice (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||Eric_PA||05-22-2001||10:22 PM||||DJRice... the sensors are Banner Mini Beams. I got them from work. They are cool to use, but at $174 list price, they are on the expensive side. I would have found something else, but they were free so why not. I was originally going to use a PIC processor, but for some reason I thought it would be different to use 7400 stuff. The object of this robot was to follow a black line over a tiled floor and be able to start in a box off of the line, then proceed onto it without any assistance. At the end of the track, the cart had to drive into the middle of a box and stop on it's own. I used the 555 Timer and set it for about 1.5 seconds. The speed of this thing with the geared motors was 11.5 inches per second. It was the fastest out of them all. Everyone else in the class used kits. To me that was too boring. I look for challenges, and using the 7400 stuff was a sure way to create a challenge. Total cost, if to buy everything for this would be $755. I enjoyed mentioning that in my presentation to the class. They thought I was nuts since they thought I actually paid for all that. I have good old work backing me up for the school stuff.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Looks like it's time to break out that old trusty breadboard and get started  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Eric<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||DJRice||05-23-2001||09:58 AM||||Yeah, banner fiber optics....Good Stuff.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||FieroJoe||05-24-2001||06:52 PM||||Well, I got it to work today!<P>I added cliff's transistor and diode setup, and got rid of most of my crappy jumper wires and used 1/16" copper tape instead.  It looks a little cleaner.<P>I did have to add a pull down resistor though.  When I was bench testing it, it would time when I gave the trigger terminal posistive voltage, and then it would time the second time when anything touched it (like what was happening before).  But, it wouldnt time again until more positive voltage was applied.  So, I thought that there was probably some extra voltage or something just hanging around, so I added a 100k pull down resistor and it works great now.  Thank you all for your help!||||reg||1
Z||000022||Cliff Pennock||05-24-2001||07:07 PM||||Where did you put the pull-<B>down</B> resistor?||||reg||1
Z||000023||FieroJoe||05-24-2001||08:54 PM||||I added it before the 47k resistor.  maybe I'm using the wrong terminology for what I did?  Bascially, I grounded out the trigger terminal with a 100k resistor and it works like it is supposed to now.<P>But wait, shouldnt that 47k resistor do the same thing?  Maybe I have that diode in backwards  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000024||Cliff Pennock||05-25-2001||02:30 AM||||The 47K resisitor is only there to protect the transisitor (and diode). If the 100K resisitor helps there, then the positive trigger is picking up static from somewhere. If it works, then leave it there. It can't do any harm.<P>I don't think you have the zener in backwards because if you had, it wouldn't work at all.||||reg||1
Z||000025||FieroJoe||05-25-2001||10:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>If it works, then leave it there. It can't do any harm.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, that's what I figured.  Well, Im just glad that it works now.  We'll see if it is more reliable than the other one I made a while ago.  All I have to do now is install it in my car.<BR>||||reg||1
